Z||000004||FieroBUZZ||05-04-2001||06:44 PM||||If the whole shifter is unattached to anything, I suspect the 'c' clip came off and may also explain the previous sloppy shifting.  I just shortened my shifter and you should be able to slid it back on the arm and reinstall the clip (in the groove). The lower cable should have a small pin through it.  My car does require a firm hand to get it in the right gear, after all, that shifter doesn't go right into the top of the tranny.<P>Gary||||reg||3
Z||000005||rogergarrison||05-04-2001||08:23 PM||||the shifters in these cars is far from the best. very sloppy, hard to feel gears. Just baby it a little after you got it working till you learn the 'feel' Ive been driving mine for 4 years and I still get em wrong once in a while (getting the 1st and 3rd mixed up). Just remember not to force it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrgone||05-04-2001||09:08 PM||||you sure? the clip came out for sure, i found it laying in there, but would the shifter be so free moving by itself? the tranny is stuck, and the gearshift can move all over the place||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bob Englert||05-04-2001||09:27 PM||||I think you need to check to see if one of the cables has broken.  Move the shifter through the gear positions and see if the cables are moving at the transmission bracket.  You could also have broken the shift cable bracket at the transmission.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
